    <div class="prose">
      <p>The standard <strong>tiny house trailer size</strong> is 8.5 ft (102 in) wide, with the finished home no taller than 13.5 ft from the road in most states, and a deck length between 16 and 32 ft for a bumper-pull trailer. Gooseneck trailers can run longer, with the combined house and gooseneck often reaching 36&ndash;40 ft. Inside those limits, the factor most people underestimate is weight: a finished 24 ft tiny house commonly weighs 10,000&ndash;12,000 lb, so the trailer&rsquo;s GVWR (gross vehicle weight rating) matters as much as its length.</p>

      <div class="key-takeaways">
        <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Stay at or under 8.5 ft wide and 13.5 ft tall to tow without oversize permits in most states. Some states allow 14 ft; check every state on your route.</li>
          <li>Common bumper-pull lengths are 16, 20, 24, 28 and 32 ft; 20&ndash;24 ft is the sweet spot for towing and livability.</li>
          <li>Budget roughly 450&ndash;550 lb per foot of trailer for a finished, furnished home, then pick a GVWR with margin to spare.</li>
          <li>A purpose-built tiny house trailer (flush deck, welded tie-downs, low deck height) saves weeks of work compared with adapting a utility or car-hauler trailer.</li>
          <li>Check your tow vehicle&rsquo;s towing capacity, hitch rating and GCWR before you buy a trailer, not after.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

