      <h2 id="legal">Legal tiny house trailer size: width, height and length</h2>
      <p>A tiny house on wheels is regulated on the road like any other trailer. The numbers below are the common limits for towing without a special permit. They vary by state, so confirm with the department of transportation in each state you will drive through.</p>
      <div class="table-wrap"><table>
        <thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Typical legal limit</th><th>What it means for your design</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Width</strong></td><td>8.5 ft (102 in), the standard limit nationwide</td><td>Measure at the widest point, including trim, eaves, window sills and lights.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Height</strong></td><td>13.5 ft (13 ft 6 in) in most states; 14 ft in some</td><td>Measured from the road to the highest point, including roof vents and chimney caps.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Trailer length</strong></td><td>Often around 40&ndash;53 ft, varies by state</td><td>Rarely the limiting factor for a tiny house on its own.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Combined length</strong></td><td>Commonly 60&ndash;65 ft truck plus trailer</td><td>Matters for long goosenecks behind crew-cab, long-bed trucks.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p>Height is where tiny houses get tight. Subtract the trailer deck height (often about 2 ft on a tiny house trailer) from 13.5 ft and you have roughly 11.5 ft for the floor, walls, loft and roof. That is why lofts in tiny homes on wheels usually have sitting headroom only, and why low-profile roofs and drop-axle trailers are popular. Our <a href="/tiny-house-on-wheels-plans/">tiny house on wheels plans</a> page covers how designs fit inside these limits, and <a href="/tiny-house-plans-with-loft/">plans with a loft</a> goes deeper on loft headroom.</p>

