      <h2 id="lengths">Tiny house trailer lengths compared</h2>
      <p>Length drives floor area, weight and how hard the home is to tow and park. The square footage below is the approximate main floor on a trailer with an 8 ft wide deck; a sleeping loft typically adds another 40&ndash;90 sq ft. Weights are typical estimates for a finished, furnished home and vary a lot with framing, siding, roofing and how much you carry.</p>
      <div class="table-wrap"><table>
        <thead><tr><th>Deck length</th><th class="num">Main floor (approx.)</th><th class="num">Typical finished weight</th><th class="num">Typical trailer GVWR</th><th>Good for</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>16 ft</td><td class="num">~128 sq ft</td><td class="num">6,000&ndash;8,000 lb</td><td class="num">7,000&ndash;10,000 lb</td><td>One person, frequent travel</td></tr>
          <tr><td>20 ft</td><td class="num">~160 sq ft</td><td class="num">8,000&ndash;10,000 lb</td><td class="num">10,000&ndash;14,000 lb</td><td>One or two people, easy towing</td></tr>
          <tr><td>24 ft</td><td class="num">~192 sq ft</td><td class="num">10,000&ndash;12,000 lb</td><td class="num">14,000 lb</td><td>Couples, full-time living</td></tr>
          <tr><td>28 ft</td><td class="num">~224 sq ft</td><td class="num">12,000&ndash;14,000 lb</td><td class="num">14,000&ndash;16,000 lb</td><td>Main-floor bedroom layouts</td></tr>
          <tr><td>32 ft</td><td class="num">~256 sq ft</td><td class="num">14,000&ndash;16,000+ lb</td><td class="num">16,000&ndash;21,000 lb</td><td>Families, rarely moved</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Gooseneck (30 ft deck + 8&ndash;10 ft neck)</td><td class="num">~300+ sq ft incl. neck</td><td class="num">14,000&ndash;18,000+ lb</td><td class="num">16,000&ndash;21,000+ lb</td><td>Raised bedroom, no ladder</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p>These are planning numbers, not a substitute for weighing your own build. Run your design through the free <a href="/tools/tiny-house-trailer-weight-calculator/">tiny house trailer weight calculator</a>, which estimates weight by length, framing, siding and contents and checks it against GVWR. Not sure how long you need? The <a href="/tools/how-big-should-my-tiny-house-be/">tiny house size calculator</a> estimates square footage from your household and storage needs, and the <a href="/blog/tiny-house-layout-ideas/">tiny house layout ideas</a> guide shows what fits on each length.</p>

      <div class="callout wood">
        <strong>The 20&ndash;24 ft sweet spot</strong>
        <p>Most people who plan to move their tiny house more than once or twice end up on a 20&ndash;24 ft trailer. It holds a full kitchen, a real bathroom and a loft, and it can usually be towed by a properly rated heavy-duty pickup. Past 28 ft, weight and maneuverability start to dominate.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="gvwr">Tiny house trailer weight ratings (GVWR)</h2>
      <p>GVWR is the maximum the trailer is rated to weigh fully loaded: trailer plus house plus everything inside. It is set by the axles, tires, frame and hitch, and you should never exceed it. Your payload, the weight of the house itself, is GVWR minus the empty trailer weight. Common ratings:</p>
      <div class="table-wrap"><table>
        <thead><tr><th class="num">GVWR</th><th>Typical axle setup</th><th class="num">Typical empty trailer</th><th class="num">Approx. payload for the house</th><th>Usually suits</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="num">7,000 lb</td><td>Two 3,500 lb axles</td><td class="num">1,800&ndash;2,500 lb</td><td class="num">~4,500&ndash;5,200 lb</td><td>Very light builds, 16 ft and under</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num">10,000 lb</td><td>Two 5,200 lb axles</td><td class="num">2,200&ndash;3,000 lb</td><td class="num">~7,000&ndash;7,800 lb</td><td>Light 16&ndash;20 ft homes</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num">14,000 lb</td><td>Two 7,000 lb axles</td><td class="num">2,800&ndash;3,800 lb</td><td class="num">~10,000&ndash;11,000 lb</td><td>Most 20&ndash;26 ft homes</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num">16,000 lb</td><td>Two 8,000 lb axles</td><td class="num">3,200&ndash;4,200 lb</td><td class="num">~12,000&ndash;12,800 lb</td><td>26&ndash;30 ft homes</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="num">21,000 lb</td><td>Three 7,000 lb axles</td><td class="num">4,000&ndash;5,500 lb</td><td class="num">~15,500&ndash;17,000 lb</td><td>30 ft+ and gooseneck homes</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p>Leave a margin of at least 10&ndash;15% under GVWR. Water in tanks, books, a full pantry and the inevitable extra tile add up. If your estimate is close to the limit, step up a rating now; upgrading axles later is expensive. Lighter choices help too: 2x4 or steel framing instead of 2x6, metal roofing instead of shingles, and lightweight siding.</p>

      <h2 id="deck">Deck-over vs drop-axle trailers</h2>
      <p>How the deck sits relative to the wheels decides how much of that 13.5 ft height you keep.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Deck-over trailers</strong> put the deck above the wheels. The floor is flat and full width with no wheel wells, but the deck sits higher, often around 30&ndash;36 in off the ground, which costs you close to a foot of interior height. Fine for a single-level home with no loft; tight for anything with a loft.</li>
        <li><strong>Standard tiny house trailers</strong> sit between the wheels with fenders or wheel wells that intrude into the floor. Decks are typically around 20&ndash;24 in high, and the wheel wells get built into cabinets, benches or closets.</li>
        <li><strong>Drop-axle trailers</strong> use axles that mount the spindle above the axle tube, lowering the deck another few inches. Every inch saved here goes into loft headroom. The trade-off is reduced ground clearance on steep driveways and rough sites.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="features">Tiny-house-specific trailer features</h2>
      <p>You can build on a converted car hauler or equipment trailer, but a trailer made for tiny houses saves time and avoids weak points. Look for:</p>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li><strong>Flush or open deck</strong> with no wood decking, so you can frame the subfloor directly on the steel and fit insulation between the cross members.</li>
        <li><strong>Welded tie-down plates or threaded anchors</strong> along the perimeter so the walls can be bolted to the frame. A tiny house on the highway sees hurricane-force wind loads.</li>
        <li><strong>Fenders sized and placed for your layout</strong>, ideally removable or designed to be framed around.</li>
        <li><strong>Electric brakes on every axle</strong> plus a breakaway system. Most states require brakes on trailers above a few thousand pounds.</li>
        <li><strong>Flashing or a moisture barrier</strong> under the subfloor to keep road spray out.</li>
        <li><strong>Leveling jacks</strong> at each corner for stable parking.</li>
        <li><strong>A VIN, title and manufacturer&rsquo;s statement of origin</strong>, which you will need to register and insure the home.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>New tiny house trailers typically cost roughly $5,000&ndash;$15,000 depending on length, GVWR and features. That is part of the overall budget in our <a href="/blog/how-much-does-a-tiny-house-cost/">tiny house cost guide</a> and the <a href="/tools/tiny-house-cost-calculator/">cost calculator</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="oversize">Going bigger: oversize permits</h2>
      <p>You can build wider than 8.5 ft or taller than your state&rsquo;s height limit, but every move then becomes an oversize load. That typically means a permit from each state you cross, &ldquo;oversize load&rdquo; banners and flags, daylight-only or weekday-only travel, and for wider loads, escort vehicles. Many owners hire a licensed hauler for the move. That is workable for a home that moves once, onto land where it will stay; it is impractical if you want to travel. If you plan to park permanently, consider whether a small home on a foundation, such as a <a href="/tiny-cottage-plans/">tiny cottage</a> or an <a href="/adu-plans/">ADU</a>, would serve you better and be easier to permit. Our <a href="/blog/tiny-house-zoning-laws/">tiny house zoning laws guide</a> explains how homes on wheels are treated compared with homes on foundations.</p>

      <h2 id="towing">Tow vehicle notes</h2>
      <p>A finished tiny house is far heavier than most travel trailers of the same length. Before you buy a trailer, look up these ratings for your truck in the owner&rsquo;s manual or on the door-jamb sticker:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Towing capacity</strong>: the maximum trailer weight the truck is rated to pull.</li>
        <li><strong>GCWR</strong> (gross combined weight rating): truck, passengers, cargo and trailer together.</li>
        <li><strong>Hitch and receiver rating</strong>: the hitch is often the weakest link. Bumper-pull tongue weight is usually around 10&ndash;15% of trailer weight.</li>
        <li><strong>Rear axle and payload rating</strong>: tongue weight counts against the truck&rsquo;s payload.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>As a rough guide, homes up to about 10,000 lb often call for a heavy-duty (three-quarter or one-ton) pickup, and heavier homes or goosenecks usually need a one-ton truck or larger. These are general patterns only; always go by your vehicle&rsquo;s actual ratings. Heavy combinations can also trigger licensing rules: some states require a special non-commercial license class or endorsement above certain combined weights, so check with your DMV. Many owners who move only once or twice simply hire a professional hauler.</p>

      <h2 id="choose">How to choose your tiny house trailer size</h2>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li><h3>Decide how often you will move</h3><p>Frequent travel points to 16&ndash;24 ft. A one-time move frees you to go longer.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Sketch the layout</h3><p>Test your must-haves in the <a href="/tools/tiny-house-layout-planner/">layout planner</a> on each length.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Estimate the weight</h3><p>Use the <a href="/tools/tiny-house-trailer-weight-calculator/">trailer weight calculator</a> and pick a GVWR with margin.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Match plans to the trailer</h3><p>Buy plans drawn for your exact trailer dimensions and wheel-well positions.</p></li>
      </ol>
      <p>Tiny Homes Blueprint can adapt a design to a specific trailer; send us the trailer&rsquo;s length, deck width, deck height and fender locations through the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>. Then follow <a href="/blog/how-to-build-a-tiny-house/">how to build a tiny house</a> for the step-by-step build.</p>

